          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Modernizing the Monarchy</strong><br>\nElizabeth II has been credited with modernizing the British monarchy. She broke with tradition by inviting commoners to her coronation and made royal finances more transparent. She also opened the doors to Buckingham Palace for tours to the public. Her adaptability has helped maintain the relevance of the monarchy in the 21st century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Family and Public Perception</strong><br>\nElizabeth's marriage to Prince Philip in 1947 was a significant event, celebrated across the Commonwealth. They have four children: Charles, Anne, Andrew, and Edward. The royal family has faced numerous public scandals, but Elizabeth has maintained high personal popularity and is seen as a stabilizing figure for the nation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Commonwealth</strong><br>\nA significant aspect of Elizabeth II's reign has been her relationship with the Commonwealth, a voluntary association of 54 sovereign states. She has stressed the importance of this international partnership and worked to keep these nations united under symbolic leadership despite the complexities of post-colonial relations and a changing global landscape.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
